<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berbers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Berber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Berbers, or Amazigh, are the indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, living across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Berber groups formed the core of several empires, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>, founded by Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> tribes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, rooted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>. They also played major roles in the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, in trans-Saharan trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, and in the hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perhaps 100,000 Arabs then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from Syria and Iraq, but the <b>Berbers</b> remained the great majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founder, Muhammad ibn Tumart, was a <b>Berber</b> theologian of the Masmuda who had studied in al-Andalus and the Islamic East and returned to the Maghreb preaching strict tawhid, the absolute unity of God, and denouncing the ruling Almoravid dynasty as anthropomorphists who ascribed physical attributes to God…</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid Empire originated in one of history's most improbable transformations: a community of Saharan <b>Berber</b> nomads, galvanised by a reformist Islamic preacher, who within some seventy years came to rule a territory stretching roughly three thousand kilometres from the Senegal River to the Ebro in northern Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Abd al-Rahman III succeeded his grandfather Abdallah in 912, the emirate was close to disintegration: the rebel Umar ibn Hafsun held the mountains around Bobastro, and Arab, <b>Berber</b> and muwallad lords ruled Seville, Toledo, Zaragoza and other cities almost independently.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He fled through Palestine and Egypt to North Africa, where he sheltered among <b>Berbers</b> related to his mother, and sent his freedman Badr to win over the Umayyad clients settled in al-Andalus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Three conditions enabled the founding: a highly organised subversive network, a militarily capable <b>Berber</b> host people, and a theological claim tracing the imamate directly to the Prophet through his daughter Fatima.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
